= Speech act =

== Overview ==
A '''speech act''' is an action performed in or by communication.

By speaking, signing, or writing, people can assert, ask, request, promise, warn, apologize, congratulate, name, declare, and perform many other socially recognizable acts.

Speech-act theory emphasizes that language is not only used to describe the world; linguistic communication can itself be action.

== Locution, illocution, and perlocution ==
J. L. Austin distinguished several aspects of an utterance.

A '''locutionary act''' concerns producing a meaningful linguistic expression.

An '''illocutionary act''' concerns what action is performed in saying it, such as asserting, requesting, or promising.

A '''perlocutionary effect''' concerns effects on an audience, such as persuading, frightening, or amusing someone.

These distinctions are useful even though later theories refine the terminology.

== Form and force ==
Sentence form does not uniquely determine speech-act force.

An interrogative can function as a question:

<blockquote>Can you reach the shelf?</blockquote>

but in another context a similar form can function as a request:

<blockquote>Can you pass the salt?</blockquote>

Therefore:

<pre>
sentence form ≠ illocutionary force
</pre>

[[Context (linguistics)|Context]], convention, intention, and social setting help determine the act.

== Felicity conditions ==
Some speech acts require appropriate circumstances.

A promise normally requires an agent capable of undertaking a commitment.

An institutional declaration may require recognized authority and procedure.

An utterance can be grammatically well formed yet fail as the intended social act if these conditions are not met.

== Direct and indirect speech acts ==
A direct speech act aligns conventional form and communicative function relatively closely.

An indirect speech act uses one form to perform another act, often through [[Pragmatics|pragmatic]] inference.

Indirectness is widespread and does not imply deception.

== Speech acts and common ground ==
Speech acts can change conversational state.

An assertion can propose information for inclusion in [[Common ground|common ground]].

A question can establish an issue to resolve.

A promise can create a commitment.

This makes speech-act theory important to discourse and formal pragmatics.

